WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.347
Because a materialized view stores rows, PostgreSQL can index those rows.

00:00:05.347 --> 00:00:09.287
This example creates a unique index on diagnosis.

00:00:09.287 --> 00:00:16.592
Indexes may support faster reads and are also relevant to certain PostgreSQL concurrent refresh requirements.

00:00:16.592 --> 00:00:24.047
Choose indexes based on real query patterns and constraints, not simply because the object is materialized.

00:00:24.047 --> 00:00:29.492
A standard view stores a query definition, not a separate copy of the rows.

00:00:29.492 --> 00:00:36.652
Treat the selected columns, names, types, and grain as a contract with downstream users.

00:00:36.652 --> 00:00:43.308
Changing that contract can break dashboards or applications even if the underlying query still runs.
